From: Ferestre contributors
Subject: Verify package header signatures

An XVD package's header is its first page, and its first 0x200 bytes are an
RSA-4096 signature over the rest of that page (0x200..0x1000): RSASSA-PSS,
SHA-256, MGF1-SHA-256, a 32-byte salt. Everything the client takes from the
header -- identity, volume flags, section sizes, the hash tree's top hash --
is in that range. Until now nothing checked it, so on a plain http CDN a
download rewritten whole (header, tree and pages together) was installed
whenever the package service gave no HashOfHashes, which is every package
seen so far.

The key that signs retail PC game headers is Microsoft's, and this client
does not carry it. `msixvc::signature` pins the SHA-256 of its
BCRYPT_RSAPUBLIC_BLOB (183F0AE0...D1229E) and verifies a header with a key
whose blob hashes to that, and with nothing else. The key is sourced on the
user's machine at run time (`xvd_key`): DisplayCatalog gives Microsoft Gaming
Services' (9MWPM2CQNLHN) Windows Update category, the Store's delivery service
(FE3: GetCookie, SyncUpdates, GetExtendedUpdateInfo2) lists its bundle and
file URL anonymously -- the ticket is an empty MSA ticket, nothing signs in --
and the bundle (about 200 MB) is streamed to a temporary file in the cache
directory, checked against its size and the SHA-256 FE3 gives, and read
without unpacking: a minimal zip64 reader opens the x64 package where it lies
in the bundle and streams its binaries (xsapi.dll and drivers/xvdd.sys first)
through a scanner that keeps only an RSA-4096 public blob with the pinned
SHA-256. The blob is stored 0600 in a 0700 directory
($XDG_DATA_HOME/xodus/xvd-key, or XODUS_XVD_KEY_DIR), hashed again on every
load, and the bundle is deleted. FE3's certificate chains to Microsoft Root
Certificate Authority 2011, which Linux stores lack and this client does not
carry: it is fetched from the CA Issuers address in FE3's intermediate
(www.microsoft.com/pki/certs, plain http), used only if its SHA-256 is the
pinned 847DF6A7...ED7C61, and is then an extra trust anchor for the FE3
client only, which is https-only, follows no redirects and talks to
fe3.delivery.mp.microsoft.com only. The answers are read with quick-xml into
a bounded tree (size, depth and element limits, no DOCTYPE, predefined
entities only). The file download is plain http from *.delivery.mp.microsoft.com;
the SHA-256 and the pin are what hold it.

`xodus-cli xvd-key [status|fetch|forget] [--json]` reports, fetches or
removes the key. It touches no account, keychain or device identity, and
prints the fingerprint, never key bytes.

`XvdFile::parse_checked` takes `HeaderChecks`: with a key, the signature is
verified over the raw header before any of it is parsed; an unsigned header
(all-zero signature) or one that does not verify is refused, a signed header
with DATA_INTEGRITY_DISABLED is refused, and with the package service's
answer the header must name that package: vduid and uduid equal to its
ContentId, pduid the trailing GUID of its VersionId, package_version the first
four fields of its Version (the relationships seen on retail packages;
product_id is not compared, its encoding is unknown). Then the top hash
(0025) and the hash tree (0022) as before. Bytes 0x1000..0x3000 are not
signed and are not relied on.

`xodus-cli streaming` uses the stored key. Without one it tries once to fetch
it, bounded at 180 s; a failed automatic attempt is not repeated for a day,
and XODUS_XVD_KEY_AUTOFETCH=0 turns automatic fetching off. With no key the
install goes on with a warning on stderr, as before, unless
XODUS_REQUIRE_SIGNED_PACKAGE=1, which refuses it. With a key, a header that
fails stops the install with an error saying it was refused, not a dropped
connection, and nothing is installed.

This is a reimplementation from the format; no XvdTool code is used. The
tests use two throwaway RSA-4096 keys generated for them
(crates/msixvc/src/testdata/TEST-ONLY-*.pem), synthetic signed headers,
zip fixtures written by the tests and hand-made FE3 answers with made-up
identifiers. Two ignored tests talk to Microsoft, anonymously: one locates the
bundle through FE3, the other checks the real key against real headers
(XODUS_LIVE_XVD_HEADERS), fetching it if it is not stored.

